# Semgrep ignore list. Every entry here is a documented, individually-
# reviewed decision, not a blanket exclusion -- mirroring the discipline
# already established in .gitleaks.toml for the same reason: a broad
# category exclusion is exactly where a real future issue could hide
# undetected.
#
# This repo's migrations are append-only by design (a bug is fixed via a
# NEW migration that supersedes the old one via CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION;
# the old migration file is never edited -- see docs/MIGRATION_CONTROL_PLANE.md).
# That means the ORIGINAL, buggy version of a function's text lives in git
# forever, even after it's fixed. A plain filesystem-wide SAST scan (unlike
# gitleaks, which we scope to new commits only) naturally scans the current
# tree's files regardless of git history, so these specific old files would
# otherwise be flagged by .semgrep/quantis-rules.yml on every single future
# CI run, forever, for a bug that is already fixed and confirmed dead live.
#
# Live-verify before trusting this list: pg_get_functiondef() against the
# actual production database is the source of truth for what's really
# running, not this file. Every function named below was independently
# confirmed live-safe via direct database query as part of QNT-DEFECT-001
# and QNT-DEFECT-015 (docs/OPEN_DEFECTS.json).

